Negative control — the SCOPE-IDENTITY seam (AC-30), the story's headline claim.

Mutates `resolveClientId`'s pin: `pinned.value ??= live` becomes
`pinned.value = live`, so the "pinned" client re-resolves off the live session
on every read. The seam is still THERE — same function, same call sites, same
`.for('client', id)` branch — it just stops holding, which is precisely the
FE-2824 shape: right structure, no capability.

Should flip RED — __tests__/client-address.scope-identity.int.test.ts:
  - "AC-30 keeps the RESOLVED client on the save URL after the session moves to
     another client mid-flight"   (the PUT goes out to OTHER_CLIENT_ID)

Should stay GREEN, and this is the point: both AC-2 tests, AND AC-30's
per-address READ — the read fires before the session moves, so it cannot
discriminate. Exactly one test in the suite can tell a held pin from a live
re-read, and this control names it. Verified: 1 failed | 3 passed.

With one live actor and no mid-flight move, "re-read the session" and "read the
seam" resolve to the same id — which is exactly why the PREVIOUS control
(deleting the `.for('client', id)` branch) flipped nothing and had to be
re-authored. The discriminator is the mid-flight session move, so the mutation
has to attack the PIN, not the branch.

